1
00:00:00,940 --> 00:00:02,956
The internet has
a graveyard.

2
00:00:02,976 --> 00:00:05,576
Not of websites, but
of entire languages

3
00:00:05,596 --> 00:00:08,296
that computers once
used to communicate.

4
00:00:08,967 --> 00:00:10,267
Before the web took over,

5
00:00:10,287 --> 00:00:11,584
there was GOPHER.

6
00:00:11,604 --> 00:00:14,204
Instead of clicking
through colorful websites,

7
00:00:14,224 --> 00:00:16,244
you navigated the
internet through simple

8
00:00:16,264 --> 00:00:17,544
text menus.

9
00:00:18,273 --> 00:00:19,413
Want to find a file?

10
00:00:19,433 --> 00:00:20,694
There was ARCHIE.

11
00:00:20,714 --> 00:00:22,754
It indexed the file
listings of public

12
00:00:22,774 --> 00:00:26,836
FTP servers, years before
web search engines.

13
00:00:26,856 --> 00:00:28,256
And WAIS went further,

14
00:00:28,276 --> 00:00:31,256
searching the text inside
the documents themselves.

15
00:00:32,092 --> 00:00:33,534
Then there was FINGER.

16
00:00:33,554 --> 00:00:35,154
It let you ask a
remote computer

17
00:00:35,174 --> 00:00:38,554
about a user, sometimes
revealing their login,

18
00:00:38,574 --> 00:00:41,174
their status, or a
personal message.

19
00:00:41,923 --> 00:00:43,265
And there were others.

20
00:00:43,285 --> 00:00:45,625
IPX, from Novell’s NetWare,

21
00:00:45,645 --> 00:00:47,345
once powered
office networks and

22
00:00:47,365 --> 00:00:48,908
multiplayer games.

23
00:00:48,928 --> 00:00:51,468
X.25 carried data across

24
00:00:51,488 --> 00:00:53,778
telecommunications
networks.

25
00:00:53,798 --> 00:00:55,718
Different machines,
different rules,

26
00:00:55,738 --> 00:00:56,978
different languages.

27
00:00:57,768 --> 00:00:59,608
But here’s the
interesting part:

28
00:00:59,628 --> 00:01:01,770
not everything
old disappeared.

29
00:01:01,790 --> 00:01:05,310
IRC and Usenet’s
NNTP still survive,

30
00:01:05,330 --> 00:01:07,050
and GOPHER still
answers on a few

31
00:01:07,070 --> 00:01:08,456
hundred servers.

32
00:01:08,476 --> 00:01:10,916
Some communities keep
using what the mainstream

33
00:01:10,936 --> 00:01:12,876
internet left behind.

34
00:01:13,755 --> 00:01:15,935
Protocols are the
hidden languages of

35
00:01:15,955 --> 00:01:17,114
the internet.

36
00:01:17,134 --> 00:01:19,374
When they disappear
from everyday use,

37
00:01:19,394 --> 00:01:21,392
their history
doesn’t vanish.

38
00:01:21,412 --> 00:01:23,332
It becomes archaeology.
